module examples.cost_arith_w

# Cost-stage golden for plans/codegen-pareto.md item C1 — type-driven W/X
# width selection (decisions 1704 / 1740 / 1741).
#
# WHY THIS CASE EXISTS. The committed corpus could not rank C1 at all.
# `cost-arith` has no multiply; `cost-mpipe-block`'s six multiplies are all
# `u64`, which is the width C1 must *not* touch. So the substitution had no
# customer anywhere in the corpus, and an unmeasured win is not a win
# (freeze 1714).
#
# WHAT IT PINS, PREDICTED FROM THE PROFILE BEFORE THE CODE WAS RUN
# (bench/a76-pi5.toml `[latency.mul]` vs `[latency.mul_w]`, SOG §3.6):
#
#   * X-form `MADD`: lat 4, thru 1/3, port M, `m_pipe_stall = 2` — holds
#     pipe M for ceil(3/1) + 2 = 5 cycles against any other M uop.
#   * W-form `MADD`: lat 2, thru 1, port M, **no** stall — holds pipe M for
#     1 cycle, because §3.6 note 4 is on the X-form row only.
#
# So the substitution is worth 2 cycles of latency per multiply, and the
# whole 4-cycle M-unit hold wherever two multiplies share a basic block.
# `wrap_mul_u32` is the shape that shows the second term: `*%` emits no
# check and therefore no branch, so all six multiplies land in one block.
#
# `wrap_mul_u64` is the control. Its declared type is 64 bits, so C1 must
# leave it on the X-form row; if this fn's Term list ever carries `mul_w`,
# the width selection has stopped being driven by the declared type.
#
# `checked_mul_u32` is the second control, and the more important one. A
# **checked** 32-bit multiply keeps the X-form even though its declared
# type is 32 bits, because its overflow test reads the high half that the
# W-form would discard — `u32 * u32` reaches 2^64 and seeing that is the
# entire point of the following bounds check. Its Term list must carry
# `mul`, never `mul_w`.

# Six wrapping 32-bit multiplies in one basic block: both the per-multiply
# latency and the M-unit hold are live here.
pub fn wrap_mul_u32(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32:
    m0: u32 = a *% b
    m1: u32 = a *% b
    m2: u32 = a *% b
    m3: u32 = a *% b
    m4: u32 = a *% b
    m5: u32 = a *% b
    return m0 +% m1 +% m2 +% m3 +% m4 +% m5

# The narrower widths take the same W-form: the declared type is what
# selects it, and every width at or below 32 bits is re-canonicalized by
# `narrow_to_width` afterwards either way.
pub fn wrap_mul_u16(a: u16, b: u16) -> u16:
    m0: u16 = a *% b
    m1: u16 = a *% b
    return m0 +% m1

pub fn wrap_mul_i16(a: i16, b: i16) -> i16:
    m0: i16 = a *% b
    m1: i16 = a *% b
    return m0 +% m1

# Control 1: 64-bit wrapping multiply keeps the X-form.
pub fn wrap_mul_u64(a: u64, b: u64) -> u64:
    m0: u64 = a *% b
    m1: u64 = a *% b
    return m0 +% m1

# Control 2: 32-bit **checked** multiply keeps the X-form, because the
# overflow check reads the bits the W-form would have thrown away.
pub fn checked_mul_u32(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32:
    return a * b
